## Decision Logging (logmind)

**This project uses [logmind](https://logmind.dev). Logging is part of the work, not after it.**

> **Tip:** the canonical procedure for *when / how / why* lives in the
> [`logmind` skill](https://github.com/thrillmot/agent-skills/tree/main/skills/logmind)
> on skills.sh. If your agent runtime supports skills, install it once
> and you can keep the project-local section here lean:
>
> ```bash
> npx skills add https://github.com/thrillmot/agent-skills --skill logmind
> ```
>
> The full procedure is also embedded below as a fallback when the
> skill isn't available.

### When you MUST log (before, not after)

Log a decision **BEFORE** you do any of these — your reasoning is the
log entry, not a justification you write afterwards:

- Choose between alternative approaches (frameworks, libraries, patterns, algorithms)
- Write more than ~20 lines of new code, or a new module
- Modify existing functionality in a non-obvious way (refactors, API changes, behaviour changes)
- Add or remove a dependency
- Make any security or performance trade-off
- Rename or move a significant module / file / function
- Choose a data shape (schema, config format, output structure)

**Do not skip logging because the change "feels small."** Small changes
that don't get logged are exactly how teams lose context. If you're
unsure, log it — a 30-second log is cheap; recovering missing context
months later is not.

**Use `logmind log` for the commit, not `git add` + `git commit`.** The
`log` command writes the decision file, stages the decision log + its
companion files (file-structure, archive if rotated), and creates the
commit in one step. Use `logmind log ... --stage all` if you want to
stage everything in the working tree alongside the decision. Bypassing
`logmind log` (manual `git commit -m "..."`) means the decision either
isn't logged or gets logged in a separate commit — both of which lose
the value of having the reasoning attached to the code change.

### How to log

**CLI (preferred):**
```bash
logmind log "Use PostgreSQL for primary database" \
  -r "Need ACID compliance and complex joins" \
  -a "MongoDB" -a "SQLite" \
  -i "Connection pooling required" \
  -i "Schema migrations via alembic"
```

**Python API:**
```python
from logmind import log

log("Use PostgreSQL for primary database",
    reasoning="Need ACID compliance and complex joins",
    alternatives=["MongoDB", "SQLite"],
    implications=["Connection pooling required", "Schema migrations via alembic"])
```

### Branch-aware routing (automatic)

On a feature branch, the entry lands in
`docs/decisions-branches/<sanitized-branch>.md`. On the default branch
it lands in `docs/decisions.md`. On PR merge, a workflow appends a
one-line summary to `docs/decisions.md` linking the PR and the
per-branch file. You don't manage this — `logmind log` does.

### Required reading at task start

Before starting any non-trivial task in this repo, **read these in order**:

1. **[docs/decisions.md](docs/decisions.md)** — 20 most recent decisions on the default branch.
2. **`docs/decisions-branches/<your-branch>.md`** if you're on a feature branch — decisions made earlier on the same branch.
3. **[docs/file-structure.md](docs/file-structure.md)** — current project tree (auto-regenerated on every log).

```bash
logmind show               # recent decisions on the current branch
logmind show --all         # include archive
logmind search "postgres"  # full-text across both files
```

Reading prior decisions first prevents you from re-litigating something
the team already decided.

### Additional reference

- **[docs/decisions-archive.md](docs/decisions-archive.md)** — historical decisions (searchable)
- **docs/decisions-branches/** — per-branch decision logs from in-flight feature work
- **README.md** at the repo root — project overview, if present
